    "lawCode": "Add §838-a, Exec L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S8117\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the executive law, in relation to\nthe processing and maintenance of sexual offense evidence kits\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo ensure that sexual offense evidence (rape kits) taken from victims\nare transferred to forensic labs and processed and entered in the\nCombined DNA Index System (CODIS) in a timely manner in order to match\nDNA against other data banks. The bill also ensures rape kits are\ntracked from police agency to labs and that reports are provided to\nDCJS on a quarterly basis.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of the bill adds a new section to the executive law\ndirecting that all sexual offense evidence kits collected and\nsurrendered to police agencies shall be submitted to a forensic\nlaboratory within 10 days of receipt and the forensic lab shall\nprocess the evidence received and communicate the results to the\nsubmitting agency and the local DA, within 90 days of receipt.\nEvidence collected prior to the effective date of this section shall\nbe submitted to labs within 180 days of the effective date and such\nlabs shall process such evidence within 120 days of receipt. The bill\ndirects forensic laboratories and police agencies to report to the\ndivision of criminal justice services quarterly on the number of kits\nreceived, tested and returned to law enforcement agencies. that a\nsecure transport of sexual offense evidence be established in order to\nreduce duplication and costs associated with each police agency\nmaintaining the chain of custody of evidence while transporting such\nevidence in a timely manner. Finally, the bill directs the division to\nensure that all agencies engaged in a law enforcement function in the\nsatte and all forensic laboratories are educated and aware of the\nprovisions of this section.\n\nSection 2 of the bill provides an effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nUnlike many states, New York does not require evidence collected from\nrape victims to be processed in a timely manner so that this\ninformation can be included in federal DNA database CODIS to help\nresolve open cases and make the important links that only DNA can.\nVictims that undergo the process of submitting sexual offense evidence\nshould have the peace of mind that this evidence will in fact be\nprocessed. Additionally, the state does not have a mechanism to track\nrape kit evidence, so we have no idea how many unprocessed kits there\nare around the state. However, recent reports suggest that many\nprecincts, some more than others, continue to have unprocessed kits on\ntheir shelves. These unprocessed kits may contain valuable information\nthat once entered into the DNA database may help solve unsolved crimes\nand exonerate the innocent.\n\nThis legislation provides a mechanism to ensure all rape kits are\nprocessed and tracked by requiring specific time frames for the\ntransmittal and processing of sexual offensive evidence collected and\nsurrendered to police agencies. It provides a longer time frame for\ntesting rape kits received prior to the new requirements to ensure\nresolving any backlog does not interfere with cases requiring priority\nprocessing. The 2016-17 adopted state budget included funding to\nassist with the timely testing, transport and reporting, and assures\nlocal law enforcement is not burdened with unfunded mandates.\n\nNew York State is a national leader. We do not have a statute of\nlimitations for the most serious sexual assaults, we have a long\nstanding process to ensure hospitals and clinics are equipped to\ncollect evidence, and we require DNA samples be given upon all felony\nand most misdemeanor convictions, meaning DNA collected in New York is\nroutinely matched against DNA in the federal data bank. There has been\na national effort to clean up backlogs of unprocessed rape kits.  In\n2003 NYC received $2.5 million from the Federal DNA Backlog Reduction\nProgram to test a backlog of approximately 17,000 rape kits resulting\nin the City's rape arrest record going up significantly. As a result\nNew York City does not have a backlog of untested kits.  While this\nwas a considerable investment in the City, we have no reliable data on\nhow many unprocessed rape kits remain in New York State as a whole.\nMany other states are making this commitment, and in 2014 District\nAttorney Cy Vance used $38 million in settlement money to help 32\njurisdictions and 20 states clean up their backlogs.  In 2015, New\nYork took new bold measures enshrining the \"enough is enough\" campaign\nagainst sexual assault on college campuses. This legislation is the\nnext step in ensuring t hat New York continues to be a national leader\nand is doing all it can to protect women, support victims and\nexonerate the innocent.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew Bill\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\n$500,000 was included in the 2016-17 adopted budget to support this\nlegislation\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThe 90th day after enactment, provided that paragraphs (c) and (d) of\nsubdivision 1 shall take effect Immediately\n",
